docs.body:
`contents(urls, options)` returns one page per requested URL, in order.

```ts
import { contents } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const { pages, cost } = await contents(["https://example.com/a", "https://example.com/b"], { provider: "exa" });
for (const page of pages) {
  if (page.status === "ok") console.log(page.title, page.content?.length);
  else console.log(page.url, page.error?.code);
}
console.log(cost.usd);
```

## Options

The call options from [Search](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/search): `provider`, `fallback`, `providerOptions`, `timeoutMs`, `attemptTimeoutMs`, `maxRetries`, `maxResponseBytes` and `signal`. Each provider takes a limited number of URLs per call; [its page](https://searchsdk.dev/providers) says how many.

## Pages

| Field | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `url` | The URL as requested |
| `status` | `ok` or `failed` |
| `finalUrl` | Address after redirects, or `null` |
| `title`, `author`, `publishedAt` | Or `null` |
| `content` | Page text, Markdown or plain per provider |
| `error` | Code and message for a failed page |

A URL the provider did not return is `failed` with code `missing`. One failed page does not fail the call.

Every response has a `cost`: `usd`, `estimated`, `source` and, for credit pricing, `credits`.

| `source` | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `reported` | The provider's response states the cost in USD |
| `reported-credits` | The provider reports credits, converted at the list price per credit |
| `computed` | Computed from the list price in the registry |
| `unknown` | No public price; `usd` is `null`, never 0 |
| `none` | Nothing was billed |

`estimated` is false only for an exact figure from the provider, or when nothing was sent. Provider options that change the price pick a price tier, or make a computed cost `unknown` when the registry cannot price them.

## Every attempt

Each entry in `attempts` has its own `cost` and `billed`: `yes` when the provider answered with success, `possible` when the request may have reached it without an answer, `no` when nothing was sent or the provider answered with an error. Retries and fallback never hide spend.

```ts
import { search } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const res = await search("sqlite wal mode", { provider: "exa" });
const billed = res.attempts.filter((a) => a.billed !== "no");
const known = billed.every((a) => a.cost.usd !== null);
const usd = billed.reduce((sum, a) => sum + (a.cost.usd ?? 0), 0);
console.log(known ? usd : "unknown");
```

A failed call carries the same `attempts` on its `SearchError`.

## Before a call

`costBound` gives the most one request can cost at the highest documented rate, such as overage or the top of a price range. Use it to hold a spend cap without guessing a margin. `listPrice` gives the computed price for a result count.

```ts
import { costBound, listPrice, registry } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const bound = costBound(registry.exa, "search", { maxResults: 10, depth: "deep" });
console.log(bound.usd); // null when it cannot be bounded; see bound.unknownBecause
console.log(listPrice(registry.exa, "search", { count: 10 }).usd);
```

A free allowance gives a bound with `requiresOverageOff: true`: it holds while paid use past the allowance stays off.

Prices come from [each provider's page](https://searchsdk.dev/providers), with the date they were read.

Both are off until you set them. Retries repeat a transient failure on the same provider; fallback moves to the next provider in your list.

```ts
import { createClient } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const client = createClient({
  provider: "exa",
  fallback: ["tavily", "brave"],
  maxRetries: 2,
  timeoutMs: 15_000,
  attemptTimeoutMs: 5_000,
});

const res = await client.search("open source vector databases");
if (res.warnings.some((w) => w.code === "fallback_used")) console.log("answered by", res.provider);
for (const a of res.attempts) console.log(a.provider, a.outcome, a.code ?? "-", a.billed);
```

## What is retried

`network`, `rate_limit`, `unavailable` and `attempt_timeout`, up to `maxRetries` times (at most 5), with jittered backoff from 250 ms to 5 s. A `Retry-After` is honoured up to 60 s; a longer one moves on. No retry starts if it would pass `timeoutMs`, which bounds the whole call.

## What falls back

The retryable codes, plus `quota` and `unsupported`. A provider without a key is skipped with a `fallback_key_missing` warning; one that cannot honour an option is skipped before anything is sent. Each provider is tried once.

`auth`, `forbidden`, `provider_error`, `invalid_response`, `spec_mismatch` and `response_too_large` stop the call: moving on would hide a bug or a billed reply. So do `timeout` and `aborted`.

## Options across a chain

Normalized options go to every provider; one that lacks an option is skipped. `providerOptions` are keyed by id, so each provider gets only its own.

```ts
import { createClient } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const client = createClient({ provider: "brave", fallback: ["exa"] });
// Brave has no startDate, so it is skipped and Exa answers.
const res = await client.search("rust 2027 edition", {
  startDate: "2026-06-01",
  providerOptions: { exa: { category: "news" } },
});
console.log(res.provider, res.attempts.map((a) => `${a.provider}:${a.outcome}`));
```

Per-call `fallback` replaces the client's list; pass `[]` to turn it off for one call. Codes: [Error codes](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/reference/errors).

searchSDK never guesses a price. A known cost is a number; an unknown one is `null`. A budget should treat `null` as "cannot approve", not as free.

## Before the call

`costBound` gives the most one request can cost at the highest documented rate. Reserve it for every attempt the call might make.

```ts
import { costBound, registry, type ProviderId } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const chain: ProviderId[] = ["exa", "tavily"];
const attemptsPerProvider = 1 + 1; // the first try plus maxRetries: 1

let reserve = 0;
for (const id of chain) {
  const bound = costBound(registry[id], "search", { maxResults: 10 });
  if (bound.usd === null) throw new Error(`${id} cannot be bounded: ${bound.unknownBecause}`);
  if (bound.requiresOverageOff) console.log(`${id}: holds only while paid overage is off`);
  reserve += bound.usd * attemptsPerProvider;
}
console.log("reserve", reserve);
```

`unknownBecause` is `option` when a provider option has no public price, `count` when the billed count is unbounded, and `price` when the price is not public.

## After the call

Sum the attempts that may have been billed. Attempts with `billed: "no"` cost nothing.

```ts
import { createClient, SearchError, type Attempt } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

function spent(attempts: readonly Attempt<string>[]): number | null {
  let usd = 0;
  for (const a of attempts.filter((x) => x.billed !== "no")) {
    if (a.cost.usd === null) return null;
    usd += a.cost.usd;
  }
  return usd;
}

const client = createClient({ provider: "exa", fallback: ["tavily"], maxRetries: 1 });
try {
  const res = await client.search("sqlite wal mode");
  console.log(spent(res.attempts), res.cost.source);
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof SearchError) console.log("failed, spent", spent(e.attempts));
}
```

`billed: "possible"` means the request may have reached the provider without an answer; count it to stay safe. A `reported` cost comes from the provider; `computed` is the registry's list price. Prices change: each [provider page](https://searchsdk.dev/providers) shows the date they were read.

One tool function serves every framework: call searchSDK, return a compact result, and return the error code instead of throwing so the model can recover.

```ts
import { createClient, SearchError } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

export const client = createClient({ provider: "exa", fallback: ["tavily"], maxRetries: 1 });

export async function webSearch(query: string) {
  try {
    const res = await client.search(query, { maxResults: 5 });
    return {
      results: res.results.map((r) => ({ title: r.title, url: r.url, publishedAt: r.publishedAt, excerpt: r.excerpts[0] ?? null })),
      provider: res.provider,
      costUsd: res.cost.usd,
    };
  } catch (e) {
    if (e instanceof SearchError) return { error: e.code };
    throw e;
  }
}
```

Keep keys on the server, cap `maxResults`, and log `cost` and `attempts` for each call.

## Vercel AI SDK

Pass the tool in `tools` to `generateText` or `streamText`.

```ts
import { tool } from "ai";
import { z } from "zod";
import { search } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

export const webSearch = tool({
  description: "Search the web. Returns titles, URLs, dates and excerpts.",
  inputSchema: z.object({ query: z.string().describe("The search query") }),
  execute: async ({ query }) => {
    const res = await search(query, { provider: "tavily", maxResults: 5 });
    return res.results.map((r) => ({ title: r.title, url: r.url, excerpt: r.excerpts[0] ?? null }));
  },
});
```

## OpenAI function calling

```ts
import OpenAI from "openai";
import { search } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const openai = new OpenAI();
const model = process.env.OPENAI_MODEL ?? "your-model-id";
const tools: OpenAI.Responses.Tool[] = [
  {
    type: "function",
    name: "web_search",
    description: "Search the web. Returns titles, URLs and excerpts.",
    parameters: { type: "object", properties: { query: { type: "string" } }, required: ["query"], additionalProperties: false },
    strict: true,
  },
];

const first = await openai.responses.create({ model, tools, input: "What changed in SQLite this year?" });
const outputs: OpenAI.Responses.ResponseInputItem[] = [];
for (const item of first.output) {
  if (item.type !== "function_call" || item.name !== "web_search") continue;
  const { query } = JSON.parse(item.arguments) as { query: string };
  const res = await search(query, { provider: "exa", maxResults: 5 });
  outputs.push({ type: "function_call_output", call_id: item.call_id, output: JSON.stringify(res.results) });
}
const answer = await openai.responses.create({ model, tools, previous_response_id: first.id, input: outputs });
console.log(answer.output_text);
```

## Anthropic tool use

```ts
import Anthropic from "@anthropic-ai/sdk";
import { search } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const anthropic = new Anthropic();
const tools: Anthropic.Tool[] = [
  {
    name: "web_search",
    description: "Search the web. Returns titles, URLs and excerpts.",
    input_schema: { type: "object", properties: { query: { type: "string" } }, required: ["query"] },
  },
];
const messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[] = [{ role: "user", content: "What changed in SQLite this year?" }];

for (;;) {
  const reply = await anthropic.messages.create({ model: "claude-opus-5-5", max_tokens: 16000, tools, messages });
  messages.push({ role: "assistant", content: reply.content });
  if (reply.stop_reason !== "tool_use") break;
  const results: Anthropic.ToolResultBlockParam[] = [];
  for (const block of reply.content) {
    if (block.type !== "tool_use") continue;
    const { query } = block.input as { query: string };
    const res = await search(query, { provider: "exa", maxResults: 5 });
    results.push({ type: "tool_result", tool_use_id: block.id, content: JSON.stringify(res.results) });
  }
  messages.push({ role: "user", content: results });
}
```

## MCP

Agents that speak MCP can use the [searchSDK MCP server](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/mcp): the local package runs `search` and `contents` with your keys, and the public endpoint serves the docs and registry.

Every failure is a `SearchError` with a fixed `code`; switch on it, never on the message.

```ts
import { search, SearchError } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

try {
  const res = await search("postgres vacuum", { provider: "tavily", maxResults: 5 });
  console.log(res.results.length);
} catch (e) {
  if (!(e instanceof SearchError)) throw e;
  switch (e.code) {
    case "configuration":
    case "invalid_request":
      console.error("fix the call:", e.field);
      break;
    case "rate_limit":
    case "quota":
    case "unavailable":
      console.error("provider trouble; retry later or add a fallback", e.retryAfterMs);
      break;
    default:
      console.error(e.code, e.provider, e.status, e.attempts);
  }
}
```

## Common causes

| Symptom | Likely cause |
| --- | --- |
| `configuration`, `field: "keys"` | The key variable is unset or empty in this process |
| `configuration`, `field: "provider"` | No provider given, or an unknown id |
| `invalid_request` | An option out of range, a bad date, or an unknown option name; `field` says which |
| `unsupported` | The provider cannot express an option; `field` says which |
| `auth` | The provider rejected the key: wrong, revoked or for another account |
| `spec_mismatch` | Our spec and the provider's reply disagree; `path` says where |
| Empty `results` | A success: the provider found nothing |

## Check before sending

`unsupported` and `invalid_request` fail before anything is sent, so they cost nothing. The [MCP server](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/mcp) tool `validate_options` runs the same checks without a key.

## Debug a provider reply

`createClient({ diagnostics })` reports each sent attempt with the request (key redacted), status, headers, a bounded body and which spec paths resolved.

```ts
import { createClient } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const client = createClient({
  provider: "exa",
  diagnostics: (event) => console.log(event.attempt.status, event.paths.filter((p) => p.missing > 0)),
});
await client.search("sqlite wal mode");
```

Report a `spec_mismatch` with its `path` as a GitHub issue. Every code: [Error codes](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/reference/errors).

A direct call builds the provider's request, reads its own response shape and handles its own errors. With searchSDK, the request, the result shape and the error codes are the same for every provider.

## Before

```ts
const response = await fetch("https://api.exa.ai/search", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "content-type": "application/json", "x-api-key": process.env.EXA_API_KEY ?? "" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ query: "sqlite wal mode", numResults: 5, category: "news" }),
});
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Exa ${response.status}`);
const body = (await response.json()) as { results: { url: string; title: string | null }[] };
console.log(body.results.map((r) => r.url));
```

## After

```ts
import { search } from "@searchsdk/sdk";

const res = await search("sqlite wal mode", {
  provider: "exa",
  maxResults: 5,
  providerOptions: { exa: { category: "news" } },
});
console.log(res.results.map((r) => r.url), res.cost.usd);
```

## Step by step

1. Install `@searchsdk/sdk` and keep the provider's key variable; searchSDK reads the same name.
2. Map the parameters that have a common name (`maxResults`, domains, dates, `country`, `language`, `depth`). [Provider support](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/reference/provider-support) shows each provider's mapping.
3. Move the rest under `providerOptions[id]`. They are validated before sending.
4. Read `results`, or `pages` for contents. Fields with no common name stay in `providerFields`, and the provider's whole body stays in `raw`.
5. Replace status checks with `SearchError` codes ([Errors](https://searchsdk.dev/docs/guides/errors)).
6. Add a `fallback` when you want a second provider.

Options a provider cannot honour fail as `unsupported` instead of being dropped, so a migrated call never silently changes meaning.

